You asked in the terminal session for two things: that CLAUDE.md tell
me to email the list when I need a dependency, and a doas rule so I
can install from FreeBSD-ports as needed. This series does both, and
declares python, which you installed in the running jail.

Patch 1 adds the doas rule. doas matches a command's arguments
exactly, so a rule for "pkg install -r FreeBSD-ports" would allow only
that line with no package names, while "cmd pkg" alone would also
allow pkg delete, -r / and the rest. Instead the rule permits one
root-owned script, /usr/local/sbin/pkg-install-ports, which runs
"pkg install -y -r FreeBSD-ports --" with the names it is given. It
also adds security/doas to the jail, and documents the script in the
README. The rule names the script by its full path, so it must be run
that way; I could not check the rule with doas -C, because doas is
not installed here yet.

Patch 2 rewrites the Dependencies section of CLAUDE.md: install from
FreeBSD-ports through that script, send a patch to instance.conf so
the jail declares the package, and ask on the list when ports cannot
supply it.

Patch 3 declares lang/python. lang/go and textproc/jq are also
installed but not declared. I left them out because I do not know
whether you meant them to stay; say so and I will add them.

The lab-process-mail prompt still says not to install missing
dependencies. That prompt is not on trunk yet, so this series cannot
change it; once both land, I will bring it in line with CLAUDE.md.


